# Cursor Coding Rules

## 1. Code Quality & Structure
- Do **not** write dummy, mock, fake, or placeholder code. Only implement real, meaningful logic.
- If a file starts to exceed **300 lines**, split it into separate modules.
- Functions and classes should remain short and focused — follow the single responsibility principle.
- For every code block you write, clearly explain:
  - Why you implemented it this way
  - What the alternatives were
  - Why you chose not to use those alternatives

## 2. Communication & Approval Flow
- If a **major system** is being added or a **large architectural change** is needed, **ask me first** — "How should we approach this?"
- Stay within the **scope of the MVP** — avoid feature creep and unnecessary expansion.
- Before writing any code, make sure you understand the **purpose of the project**. If in doubt, ask for clarification.

## 3. File & Folder Organization
- Place all executable scripts (CLI tools, startup files, etc.) in the `scripts/` directory — **never in the project root**.
- Place all test files in the `test/` directory.
- Avoid clutter in the root directory: no throwaway files, test files, or scripts outside their respective folders.
- Do not write speculative "for later" utilities or boilerplate unless they are immediately necessary.

## 4. Managing File Size & Complexity
- If a file grows too large (especially controllers or classes), split it cleanly and modularly.
- Use folders like `utils/`, `services/`, `models/`, `components/` **only when needed**, not preemptively.

## 5. Explanation Requirement
For **every code block**, you must explain:
  - What it does
  - Why it is implemented this way
  - What other approaches were considered and why this one was chosen
